Mycoplasma genitalium
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Persistent watery urethral/vaginal discharge, Severe burning dysuria, Refractory to standard empiric antibiotics, Positive specific NAAT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-031
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe dysuria and pelvic pain, unresponsive to standard STI treatments, requiring urgent assessment for complicated or resistant pelvic inflammatory disease.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing severe, persistent burning upon urination and new-onset deep pelvic pain, despite recently completing standard first-line antibiotic therapy for a presumed STI. Urgent physical assessment is required to rule out an evolving pelvic abscess, ectopic pregnancy, and to initiate targeted second-line therapy prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It still burns like acid when I pee, the first antibiotics did absolutely nothing, and now I have a deep ache in my lower stomach."
History of Present Illness: Illness initially commenced 3 weeks ago with severe dysuria characterized by a persistent burning and intense stinging sensation during micturition, accompanied by a persistent, distinct watery vaginal discharge. The patient was treated empirically at a walk-in clinic 10 days ago with standard first-line antibiotics (Doxycycline) for presumed Chlamydia, but symptoms persisted entirely unchanged. Over the last 48 hours, the patient has developed new-onset deep pelvic pain and severe lower abdominal discomfort. She also reports recent intermenstrual bleeding and exquisite, deep pain during sexual intercourse (dyspareunia).
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but resting in bed due to pelvic discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: High frustration and anxiety regarding the failure of the initial treatment and the escalating pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, mildly distressed, anxious regarding the ongoing symptoms.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals mild bilateral lower quadrant tenderness with voluntary guarding; no rebound or rigidity. Bowel sounds normal. Speculum exam (performed with consent) reveals a watery discharge and a friable, inflamed cervix that distinctively bleeds upon minimal contact. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Persistent watery urethral/vaginal discharge, Severe burning dysuria, Refractory to standard empiric antibiotics, and Positive specific NAAT (pending targeted swab).
Environmental Observations: Patient has the empty packet of previously prescribed Doxycycline on the counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently completed a 7-day course of oral Doxycycline with zero clinical improvement. No other regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mycoplasma genitalium (Mgen) - complicated by early Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Neisseria gonorrhoeae (Resistant strain) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The discharge is watery rather than the classic thick, copious purulent (green/yellow) discharge typical of Gonorrhea, though co-infection or resistance is possible.

Trichomoniasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a frothy, malodorous, yellow-green discharge and "strawberry cervix," and the distinct failure of broad-spectrum therapy.

Urinary Tract Infection (complicated) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent presence of vaginal discharge, a friable cervix, and deep dyspareunia, which indicate a genital rather than purely urinary tract pathology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, escalating pelvic pain and dysuria that has completely failed standard syndromic STI management. This clinical trajectory—specifically the failure of standard empirical Doxycycline—is highly suspicious for Mycoplasma genitalium, a pathogen notorious for macrolide and tetracycline resistance. The emergence of deep pelvic pain suggests ascending infection (PID). Immediate on-site clinical evaluation was mandatory to rule out surgical pelvic emergencies, physically assess the cervix, and immediately initiate targeted, sequential second-line antibiotic therapy (e.g., Moxifloxacin) to prevent permanent tubal damage. This presentation strictly satisfies the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe specific second-line therapy for presumed Mgen PID (e.g., Moxifloxacin 400mg daily for 14 days, pending local resistance guidelines). Urgent referral for a high vaginal swab and first-catch urine specifically requesting Mgen NAAT and macrolide resistance testing. Provide strong oral analgesia (NSAIDs).
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the Moxifloxacin exactly at the same time every morning with a large glass of water, the patient will maintain optimal, steady blood levels of the antibiotic; the resulting steady decrease in the burning sensation will reward the patient, reinforcing strict daily compliance with this potent medication.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP or Sexual Health Clinic review in 72 hours to assess clinical response and review the specific Mgen NAAT results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops intractable vomiting, an inability to tolerate the oral antibiotics, high fever exceeding 39.0C, or if the pelvic pain becomes suddenly agonizing and unrelenting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending specific molecular testing; antibiotic resistance patterns are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Mgen is a difficult-to-treat bacteria that lacks a standard cell wall, making many common antibiotics useless. The new antibiotic targets the bacteria's DNA directly. Pain should begin to subside within 3-4 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Because this infection has already proven resistant to the first round of drugs, we must monitor you closely. Seek immediate emergency care if the stomach pain suddenly doubles in intensity or if you start vomiting and cannot keep the new pills down.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The first antibiotic failed because this specific bacteria (Mycoplasma) is essentially immune to it. We are now upgrading you to a much stronger class of antibiotic. It is critical that you finish every single pill of this new prescription to prevent the bacteria from developing further resistance and causing permanent scarring.
Actionable Steps: Do not take antacids (like QuickEze or Mylanta) or iron/calcium supplements within 4 hours of taking the Moxifloxacin, as they will stop the antibiotic from absorbing. Abstain completely from sexual contact.

